{
  "email": "sofia.rossi@brightline.example",
  "reason": "Ticket sender asks about laptop check-in alert after two-week leave. Need account and assigned devices per KB-018."
}

{
  "hostname": "BL-LT-0107",
  "reason": "Check online state, last check-in and warnings for user's assigned laptop per KB-018."
}

{
  "query": "device not checking in after leave laptop offline management",
  "reason": "Confirm no better-matching article than KB-018 for an offline laptop after extended leave."
}

{
  "outcome": "needs_user_info",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Sofia, welcome back. The check-in alert isn't a problem in itself. Your laptop (BL-LT-0107) has been offline for about 12 days, which is normal after a leave, but it means it has not received updates or security policies during that time. To bring it back in line, please power on the laptop, connect it to the internet (office Wi-Fi, home Wi-Fi, or a wired connection), and leave it signed in for about 30 minutes. Then restart it. Once it has checked in, the alert should clear. If the alert is still showing after that, reply to this ticket and we'll take a closer look.",
  "internal_note": "Sofia Rossi (user_id 7), active, not blocked, Marketing. Assigned device BL-LT-0107 (Win 11, laptop): online=false, last_checkin 2026-09-29 (about 12 days ago), warning 'No check-in for 12 days', disk 54.7% free. Per KB-018 this is past the 7-day follow-up threshold, but the user is active and the device is assigned to her, so this is not a former-employee or unreturned-device case. Asked user to power on, connect, stay signed in 30 min, and restart. Next step: re-run check_device_status after user confirms. Escalate to Tier 2 under KB-018 if the device is online but still not checking in after a restart. No actions queued. Note the ticket is P4 with medium-confidence triage; no security concern seen (no failed logins, sign-in not blocked).",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-018"
  ],
  "confidence": "high"
}
